To make a block accept nested “group” blocks you simply add an **InnerBlocks** component to the block’s edit function.  
`InnerBlocks` renders the inserter and the placeholder that lets users add any block that is allowed inside this container.  
You can limit what can be inserted by passing an `allowedBlocks` (or `allowedBlockTypes`) prop, or by providing a `template`. For example:

```js
import { InnerBlocks } from '@wordpress/block-editor';

export default function Edit( { attributes, setAttributes } ) {
    return (
        <div className="my-block">
            {/* …your block markup… */}
            <InnerBlocks
                allowedBlocks={ [ 'core/group', 'core/paragraph', 'core/image' ] }
                template={ [ [ 'core/group', {} ], [ 'core/paragraph', {} ] ] }
                templateLock={ false }          // allow rearranging/editing
            />
        </div>
    );
}
```

With `InnerBlocks` in place, the block editor will automatically show the “+” button inside your block, letting users drop and configure child group blocks (or any other allowed block types). No other changes are required in the edit function beyond that component, although you should still register
